{"post_stream": {"posts": [{"id": 261281, "name": "Kees de Jong", "username": "keesdejong", "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/discussion.fedoraproject.org/keesdejong/{size}/1922_2.png", "created_at": "2023-12-08T19:39:01.751Z", "cooked": "<p><a href=\"https://github.com/fedora-infra/the-new-hotness/issues/586#issuecomment-1810208403\">In this Github issue</a> a question is put forward, which can be summarized as:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>I’m the maintainer of the neofetch package for Fedora. Is changing the upstream repo for an existing package a common method? I know it’s a fork of neofetch, but it continues under a different name, right? Wouldn’t it make more sense to start a new package called hyfetch with the new repo? Then we can just retire neofetch. I don’t have the time or interest to package hyfetch, so if someone else wants to package that one. There is also <a href=\"https://github.com/fastfetch-cli/fastfetch\">fastfetch</a>.</p>\n<p>In case the Fedora dev community thinks it’s a good idea to switch to a different upstream source, but keep the name of the original package name, then I’ll fix that and continue. But I personally think it’s messy. What if the original maintainer of neofetch starts developing again?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>My solution would be to start a new package, and if <code>neofetch</code> is really unmaintained, the package can be retired. With maybe something in the spec file of <code>hyfetch</code> that it replaces <code>neofetch</code>?</p>", "post_number": 1, "post_type": 1, "posts_count": 7, "updated_at": "2023-12-08T19:39:31.613Z", "reply_count": 1, "reply_to_post_number": null, "quote_count": 0, "incoming_link_count": 151, "reads": 40, "readers_count": 39, "score": 813.0, "yours": false, "topic_id": 98545, "topic_slug": "how-does-the-new-hotness-handle-a-repo-change", "display_username": "Kees de Jong", "primary_group_name": null, "flair_name": null, "flair_url": null, "flair_bg_color": null, "flair_color": null, "flair_group_id": null, "badges_granted": [], "version": 1, "can_edit": false, "can_delete": false, "can_recover": false, "can_see_hidden_post": false, "can_wiki": false, "link_counts": [{"url": "https://github.com/fastfetch-cli/fastfetch", "internal": false, "reflection": false, "title": "GitHub - fastfetch-cli/fastfetch: Like neofetch, but much faster because written in C.", "clicks": 7}, {"url": "https://github.com/fedora-infra/the-new-hotness/issues/586#issuecomment-1810208403", "internal": false, "reflection": false, "title": "How does the-new-hotness handle a repo change? · Issue #586 · fedora-infra/the-new-hotness · GitHub", "clicks": 6}], "read": true, "user_title": "Packaging Team", "title_is_group": false, "bookmarked": false, "actions_summary": [{"id": 2, "count": 1}], "moderator": false, "admin": false, "staff": false, "user_id": 778, "hidden": false, "trust_level": 2, "deleted_at": null, "user_deleted": false, "edit_reason": null, "can_view_edit_history": true, "wiki": false, "mentioned_users": [], "post_url": "/t/how-does-the-new-hotness-handle-a-repo-change/98545/1", "event": null, "calendar_details": [], "reactions": [{"id": "heart", "type": "emoji", "count": 1}], "current_user_reaction": null, "reaction_users_count": 1, "current_user_used_main_reaction": false, "can_accept_answer": false, "can_unaccept_answer": false, "accepted_answer": false, "topic_accepted_answer": true}, {"id": 267703, "name": "Kees de Jong", "username": "keesdejong", "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/discussion.fedoraproject.org/keesdejong/{size}/1922_2.png", "created_at": "2024-01-09T13:20:19.574Z", "cooked": "<p>Any (strong) opinions about this issue? I keep getting automated Bugzilla tickets on my name because I suppose someone set up this release monitoring for my package. But I personally think it’s wrong. The forked project should be packaged and if <code>neofetch</code> is really unmaintained, it should be orphaned. And perhaps the new <code>hyfetch</code> package can be configured to provide the virtual <code>neofetch</code> package as well?</p>", "post_number": 2, "post_type": 1, "posts_count": 7, "updated_at": "2024-01-09T13:20:19.574Z", "reply_count": 0, "reply_to_post_number": null, "quote_count": 0, "incoming_link_count": 0, "reads": 31, "readers_count": 30, "score": 6.2, "yours": false, "topic_id": 98545, "topic_slug": "how-does-the-new-hotness-handle-a-repo-change", "display_username": "Kees de Jong", "primary_group_name": null, "flair_name": null, "flair_url": null, "flair_bg_color": null, "flair_color": null, "flair_group_id": null, "badges_granted": [], "version": 1, "can_edit": false, "can_delete": false, "can_recover": false, "can_see_hidden_post": false, "can_wiki": false, "read": true, "user_title": "Packaging Team", "title_is_group": false, "bookmarked": false, "actions_summary": [], "moderator": false, "admin": false, "staff": false, "user_id": 778, "hidden": false, "trust_level": 2, "deleted_at": null, "user_deleted": false, "edit_reason": null, "can_view_edit_history": true, "wiki": false, "mentioned_users": [], "post_url": "/t/how-does-the-new-hotness-handle-a-repo-change/98545/2", "event": null, "reactions": [], "current_user_reaction": null, "reaction_users_count": 0, "current_user_used_main_reaction": false, "can_accept_answer": false, "can_unaccept_answer": false, "accepted_answer": false, "topic_accepted_answer": true}, {"id": 269943, "name": "ilikelinux", "username": "ilikelinux", "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/discussion.fedoraproject.org/ilikelinux/{size}/27169_2.png", "created_at": "2024-01-20T20:11:46.390Z", "cooked": "<p>As long as the package has a maintainer (you) it will not get retired.</p>\n<p>Just try to get rid of the maintainer job for this package. If no body takes over the package gets retired because of missing maintainer.</p>\n<p>And yes fastfetch is much faster, and in my opinion it does the same.</p>", "post_number": 3, "post_type": 1, "posts_count": 7, "updated_at": "2024-01-20T20:11:46.390Z", "reply_count": 1, "reply_to_post_number": null, "quote_count": 0, "incoming_link_count": 0, "reads": 27, "readers_count": 26, "score": 10.4, "yours": false, "topic_id": 98545, "topic_slug": "how-does-the-new-hotness-handle-a-repo-change", "display_username": "ilikelinux", "primary_group_name": "ask-fedora", "flair_name": "ask-fedora", "flair_url": null, "flair_bg_color": "", "flair_color": "", "flair_group_id": 53, "badges_granted": [], "version": 1, "can_edit": false, "can_delete": false, "can_recover": false, "can_see_hidden_post": false, "can_wiki": false, "read": true, "user_title": "Let's have a party (Fedora 44)", "title_is_group": false, "bookmarked": false, "actions_summary": [], "moderator": false, "admin": false, "staff": false, "user_id": 2081, "hidden": false, "trust_level": 2, "deleted_at": null, "user_deleted": false, "edit_reason": null, "can_view_edit_history": true, "wiki": false, "mentioned_users": [], "post_url": "/t/how-does-the-new-hotness-handle-a-repo-change/98545/3", "event": null, "reactions": [], "current_user_reaction": null, "reaction_users_count": 0, "current_user_used_main_reaction": false, "can_accept_answer": false, "can_unaccept_answer": false, "accepted_answer": false, "topic_accepted_answer": true}, {"id": 270170, "name": "Kees de Jong", "username": "keesdejong", "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/discussion.fedoraproject.org/keesdejong/{size}/1922_2.png", "created_at": "2024-01-22T11:07:22.889Z", "cooked": "<p>True, that’s an option, to retire the package. But I’m a bit confused why the release monitoring is now tied to a forked project of neofetch. I will not release neofetch with code from another project. If neofetch for whatever reason gets back on track, then I have a version issue.</p>\n<p>But I guess I can just ignore these automatic tickets that are raised by the release monitoring. I also find it strange that someone else messed with this. Because I didn’t change the release monitoring.</p>", "post_number": 4, "post_type": 1, "posts_count": 7, "updated_at": "2024-01-22T11:07:22.889Z", "reply_count": 1, "reply_to_post_number": 3, "quote_count": 0, "incoming_link_count": 0, "reads": 22, "readers_count": 21, "score": 9.4, "yours": false, "topic_id": 98545, "topic_slug": "how-does-the-new-hotness-handle-a-repo-change", "display_username": "Kees de Jong", "primary_group_name": null, "flair_name": null, "flair_url": null, "flair_bg_color": null, "flair_color": null, "flair_group_id": null, "badges_granted": [], "version": 1, "can_edit": false, "can_delete": false, "can_recover": false, "can_see_hidden_post": false, "can_wiki": false, "read": true, "user_title": "Packaging Team", "title_is_group": false, "reply_to_user": {"id": 2081, "username": "ilikelinux", "name": "ilikelinux", "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/discussion.fedoraproject.org/ilikelinux/{size}/27169_2.png"}, "bookmarked": false, "actions_summary": [], "moderator": false, "admin": false, "staff": false, "user_id": 778, "hidden": false, "trust_level": 2, "deleted_at": null, "user_deleted": false, "edit_reason": null, "can_view_edit_history": true, "wiki": false, "mentioned_users": [], "post_url": "/t/how-does-the-new-hotness-handle-a-repo-change/98545/4", "event": null, "reactions": [], "current_user_reaction": null, "reaction_users_count": 0, "current_user_used_main_reaction": false, "can_accept_answer": false, "can_unaccept_answer": false, "accepted_answer": false, "topic_accepted_answer": true}, {"id": 270172, "name": "FranciscoD", "username": "ankursinha", "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/discussion.fedoraproject.org/ankursinha/{size}/71_2.png", "created_at": "2024-01-22T11:23:20.239Z", "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote no-group\" data-username=\"keesdejong\" data-post=\"1\" data-topic=\"98545\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://sea1.discourse-cdn.com/fedoraproject/user_avatar/discussion.fedoraproject.org/keesdejong/48/1922_2.png\" class=\"avatar\"> Kees de Jong:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>My solution would be to start a new package, and if <code>neofetch</code> is really unmaintained, the package can be retired. With maybe something in the spec file of <code>hyfetch</code> that it replaces <code>neofetch</code>?</p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>This case is a little tricky, because <code>hyfetch</code> is quite clearly a fork that builds on the neofetch sources, provide <code>neofetch</code>, and is intended to replace it:</p>\n<aside class=\"onebox allowlistedgeneric\" data-onebox-src=\"https://github.com/hykilpikonna/hyfetch\">\n  <header class=\"source\">\n      <img src=\"https://us1.discourse-cdn.com/fedoraproject/original/2X/b/bad3e5f9ad67c1ddf145107ce7032ac1d7b22563.svg\" class=\"site-icon\" data-dominant-color=\"\" width=\"32\" height=\"32\">\n\n      <a href=\"https://github.com/hykilpikonna/hyfetch\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">GitHub</a>\n  </header>\n\n  <article class=\"onebox-body\">\n    <div class=\"aspect-image\" style=\"--aspect-ratio:690/344;\"><img src=\"https://us1.discourse-cdn.com/fedoraproject/optimized/3X/a/9/a90c4415dbd58c7de5719a5b490cf384aadffd7b_2_690x345.png\" class=\"thumbnail\" data-dominant-color=\"F2F4F3\" width=\"690\" height=\"345\"></div>\n\n<h3><a href=\"https://github.com/hykilpikonna/hyfetch\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">GitHub - hykilpikonna/hyfetch: 🏳️‍🌈 🏳️‍⚧️ Neofetch with LGBTQ+ pride flags!</a></h3>\n\n  <p>🏳️‍🌈 🏳️‍⚧️ Neofetch with LGBTQ+ pride flags! Contribute to hykilpikonna/hyfetch development by creating an account on GitHub.</p>\n\n\n  </article>\n\n  <div class=\"onebox-metadata\">\n    \n    \n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"clear: both\"></div>\n</aside>\n\n<blockquote>\n<p>This repo also serves as an updated version of the original <code>neofetch</code> since the upstream <a href=\"https://github.com/dylanaraps/neofetch\">dylanaraps/neofetch</a> doesn’t seem to be maintained anymore (as of Oct 27, 2023, the original repo hasn’t merged a pull request for almost 2 years).</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Anyone can update things on release-monitoring.org—that’s a public resource. This means there can be multiple people modifying the information. For example, in Fedora, you may want to retire neofetch and have hyfetch etc. obsolete it, but other distros may keep the neofetch name but use the hyfetch sources.</p>\n<p>In this case, since hyfetch does provide <code>neofetch</code>, the latter is also OK. I know there’s always the risk of an inactive project returning, but if neofetch hasn’t been active in a number of years now, it is safe enough to use a fork. If it does return, the fork can be merged back in etc. to continue the versioning (but no PRs have been merged into neofetch in a number of years either).  Versioning can also be fixed downstream using <code>Epochs</code> and so on, so there are options.</p>\n<p><code>fastfetch</code> does not say it’s an updated neofetch, so that should be a different package:</p>\n<aside class=\"onebox allowlistedgeneric\" data-onebox-src=\"https://github.com/fastfetch-cli/fastfetch\">\n  <header class=\"source\">\n      <img src=\"https://us1.discourse-cdn.com/fedoraproject/original/2X/b/bad3e5f9ad67c1ddf145107ce7032ac1d7b22563.svg\" class=\"site-icon\" data-dominant-color=\"\" width=\"32\" height=\"32\">\n\n      <a href=\"https://github.com/fastfetch-cli/fastfetch\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">GitHub</a>\n  </header>\n\n  <article class=\"onebox-body\">\n    <div class=\"aspect-image\" style=\"--aspect-ratio:690/345;\"><img src=\"https://us1.discourse-cdn.com/fedoraproject/optimized/3X/3/f/3f4b3f6ad3701780acd79cb5189f3e4c9202ac2d_2_690x345.png\" class=\"thumbnail\" data-dominant-color=\"050808\" width=\"690\" height=\"345\"></div>\n\n<h3><a href=\"https://github.com/fastfetch-cli/fastfetch\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">GitHub - fastfetch-cli/fastfetch: Like neofetch, but much faster because...</a></h3>\n\n  <p>Like neofetch, but much faster because written mostly in C. - GitHub - fastfetch-cli/fastfetch: Like neofetch, but much faster because written mostly in C.</p>\n\n\n  </article>\n\n  <div class=\"onebox-metadata\">\n    \n    \n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"clear: both\"></div>\n</aside>\n\n<blockquote>\n<p>Fastfetch is a <a href=\"https://github.com/dylanaraps/neofetch\">neofetch</a>-like tool for fetching system information and displaying them in a pretty way. It is written mainly in C, with performance and customizability in mind. Currently, Linux, Android, FreeBSD, MacOS and Windows 7+ are supported.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>If you don’t want bugzilla tickets about new releases, you can just disable monitoring at <a href=\"https://src.fedoraproject.org/rpms/neofetch\" class=\"inline-onebox\">Overview - rpms/neofetch - src.fedoraproject.org</a> (see the left hand side bar).</p>\n<p>Ultimately, the question of whether hyfetch is packaged separately to obsolete neofetch, or if you keep neofetch and just use the hyfetch sources, is up to you, the neofetch maintainer. If you’d rather a new package, that is a perfectly correct way to go <img src=\"https://emoji.discourse-cdn.com/twitter/slight_smile.png?v=12\" title=\":slight_smile:\" class=\"emoji\" alt=\":slight_smile:\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"20\" height=\"20\"></p>\n<aside class=\"onebox allowlistedgeneric\" data-onebox-src=\"https://docs.fedoraproject.org/en-US/packaging-guidelines/#renaming-or-replacing-existing-packages\">\n  <header class=\"source\">\n\n      <a href=\"https://docs.fedoraproject.org/en-US/packaging-guidelines/#renaming-or-replacing-existing-packages\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Fedora Docs</a>\n  </header>\n\n  <article class=\"onebox-body\">\n    <div class=\"aspect-image\" style=\"--aspect-ratio:225/62;\"><img src=\"https://us1.discourse-cdn.com/fedoraproject/original/2X/4/4475ce42a869f70b1d515bd1da99681add9ce512.png\" class=\"thumbnail\" data-dominant-color=\"4E7EAE\" width=\"225\" height=\"62\"></div>\n\n<h3><a href=\"https://docs.fedoraproject.org/en-US/packaging-guidelines/#renaming-or-replacing-existing-packages\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Fedora Packaging Guidelines</a></h3>\n\n  <p>Learn more about Fedora Linux, the Fedora Project &amp; the Fedora Community.</p>\n\n\n  </article>\n\n  <div class=\"onebox-metadata\">\n    \n    \n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"clear: both\"></div>\n</aside>\n", "post_number": 5, "post_type": 1, "posts_count": 7, "updated_at": "2024-01-22T11:23:20.239Z", "reply_count": 1, "reply_to_post_number": 4, "quote_count": 1, "incoming_link_count": 0, "reads": 22, "readers_count": 21, "score": 39.4, "yours": false, "topic_id": 98545, "topic_slug": "how-does-the-new-hotness-handle-a-repo-change", "display_username": "FranciscoD", "primary_group_name": null, "flair_name": null, "flair_url": null, "flair_bg_color": null, "flair_color": null, "flair_group_id": null, "badges_granted": [], "version": 1, "can_edit": false, "can_delete": false, "can_recover": false, "can_see_hidden_post": false, "can_wiki": false, "link_counts": [{"url": "https://github.com/hykilpikonna/hyfetch", "internal": false, "reflection": false, "title": "GitHub - hykilpikonna/hyfetch: 🏳️‍🌈 🏳️‍⚧️ Neofetch with LGBTQ+ pride flags!", "clicks": 3}, {"url": "https://github.com/fastfetch-cli/fastfetch", "internal": false, "reflection": false, "title": "GitHub - fastfetch-cli/fastfetch: Like neofetch, but much faster because written mostly in C.", "clicks": 1}, {"url": "https://docs.fedoraproject.org/en-US/packaging-guidelines/#renaming-or-replacing-existing-packages", "internal": false, "reflection": false, "title": "Fedora Packaging Guidelines :: Fedora Docs", "clicks": 0}, {"url": "https://src.fedoraproject.org/rpms/neofetch", "internal": false, "reflection": false, "title": "Overview - rpms/neofetch - src.fedoraproject.org", "clicks": 0}, {"url": "https://github.com/dylanaraps/neofetch", "internal": false, "reflection": false, "title": "GitHub - dylanaraps/neofetch: 🖼️ A command-line system information tool written in bash 3.2+", "clicks": 0}, {"url": "https://discussion.fedoraproject.org/t/neofetch-alternative/117109/6", "internal": true, "reflection": true, "title": "Neofetch alternative", "clicks": 0}], "read": true, "user_title": "", "reply_to_user": {"id": 778, "username": "keesdejong", "name": "Kees de Jong", "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/discussion.fedoraproject.org/keesdejong/{size}/1922_2.png"}, "bookmarked": false, "actions_summary": [{"id": 2, "count": 2}], "moderator": true, "admin": false, "staff": true, "user_id": 78, "hidden": false, "trust_level": 3, "deleted_at": null, "user_deleted": false, "edit_reason": null, "can_view_edit_history": true, "wiki": false, "mentioned_users": [], "post_url": "/t/how-does-the-new-hotness-handle-a-repo-change/98545/5", "event": null, "reactions": [{"id": "heart", "type": "emoji", "count": 2}], "current_user_reaction": null, "reaction_users_count": 2, "current_user_used_main_reaction": false, "can_accept_answer": false, "can_unaccept_answer": false, "accepted_answer": true, "topic_accepted_answer": true}, {"id": 272219, "name": "Kees de Jong", "username": "keesdejong", "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/discussion.fedoraproject.org/keesdejong/{size}/1922_2.png", "created_at": "2024-01-28T12:04:03.640Z", "cooked": "<p>Thanks for the detailed answer! I think I will switch the upstream source to this hyfetch fork. But first I’ll inspect the fork a bit better and see if there is a way to provide both hyfetch and neofetch with the same package. I believe this is possible, I’ve seen it before. But I’ll have to go through the RPM dev docs again.</p>\n<p>Edit: I just had a look at the hyfetch project. It’s more than just a fork, they seem to be moving to a more Python based application. I’ll orphan neofetch in that case and someone else may package hyfetch and go through a new package review. I think I’ll orphan it sometime later this year. Just in case any activity sparks up for neofetch. If not, then I suppose Fedora 41 won’t have neofetch anymore.</p>\n<p>In case someone wants to package hyfetch, here’s an example:</p>\n<aside class=\"onebox allowlistedgeneric\" data-onebox-src=\"https://build.opensuse.org/package/view_file/openSUSE:Slowroll:Base/hyfetch/hyfetch.spec?expand=1\">\n  <header class=\"source\">\n      <img src=\"https://us1.discourse-cdn.com/fedoraproject/original/3X/5/0/50849340679e316b67863805f594e96f5f72e673.png\" class=\"site-icon\" data-dominant-color=\"7D858D\" width=\"64\" height=\"64\">\n\n      <a href=\"https://build.opensuse.org/package/view_file/openSUSE:Slowroll:Base/hyfetch/hyfetch.spec?expand=1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow ugc\">openSUSE Build Service</a>\n  </header>\n\n  <article class=\"onebox-body\">\n    <div class=\"aspect-image\" style=\"--aspect-ratio:690/315;\"><img src=\"https://us1.discourse-cdn.com/fedoraproject/original/3X/6/6/66fef10fe54b57a5033a2788ed9289e58cf62d3f.png\" class=\"thumbnail\" data-dominant-color=\"808080\" width=\"690\" height=\"315\"></div>\n\n<h3><a href=\"https://build.opensuse.org/package/view_file/openSUSE:Slowroll:Base/hyfetch/hyfetch.spec?expand=1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow ugc\">openSUSE Build Service</a></h3>\n\n\n\n  </article>\n\n  <div class=\"onebox-metadata\">\n    \n    \n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"clear: both\"></div>\n</aside>\n", "post_number": 6, "post_type": 1, "posts_count": 7, "updated_at": "2024-01-28T13:03:46.772Z", "reply_count": 1, "reply_to_post_number": 5, "quote_count": 0, "incoming_link_count": 0, "reads": 18, "readers_count": 17, "score": 53.6, "yours": false, "topic_id": 98545, "topic_slug": "how-does-the-new-hotness-handle-a-repo-change", "display_username": "Kees de Jong", "primary_group_name": null, "flair_name": null, "flair_url": null, "flair_bg_color": null, "flair_color": null, "flair_group_id": null, "badges_granted": [], "version": 2, "can_edit": false, "can_delete": false, "can_recover": false, "can_see_hidden_post": false, "can_wiki": false, "link_counts": [{"url": "https://build.opensuse.org/package/view_file/openSUSE:Slowroll:Base/hyfetch/hyfetch.spec?expand=1", "internal": false, "reflection": false, "title": "File hyfetch.spec of Package hyfetch - openSUSE Build Service", "clicks": 0}], "read": true, "user_title": "Packaging Team", "title_is_group": false, "reply_to_user": {"id": 78, "username": "ankursinha", "name": "FranciscoD", "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/discussion.fedoraproject.org/ankursinha/{size}/71_2.png"}, "bookmarked": false, "actions_summary": [{"id": 2, "count": 1}], "moderator": false, "admin": false, "staff": false, "user_id": 778, "hidden": false, "trust_level": 2, "deleted_at": null, "user_deleted": false, "edit_reason": null, "can_view_edit_history": true, "wiki": false, "mentioned_users": [], "post_url": "/t/how-does-the-new-hotness-handle-a-repo-change/98545/6", "event": null, "reactions": [{"id": "heart", "type": "emoji", "count": 1}], "current_user_reaction": null, "reaction_users_count": 1, "current_user_used_main_reaction": false, "can_accept_answer": false, "can_unaccept_answer": false, "accepted_answer": false, "topic_accepted_answer": true}, {"id": 298546, "name": "Kees de Jong", "username": "keesdejong", "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/discussion.fedoraproject.org/keesdejong/{size}/1922_2.png", "created_at": "2024-05-01T08:34:05.284Z", "cooked": "<p>I’ll orphan the package this week.</p>\n<aside class=\"onebox allowlistedgeneric\" data-onebox-src=\"https://www.omgubuntu.co.uk/2024/04/neofetch-system-info-tool-is-dead\">\n  <header class=\"source\">\n      <img src=\"https://us1.discourse-cdn.com/fedoraproject/original/3X/e/0/e05f2bf23e8c56b0ed12b26460859805cfa8db0e.png\" class=\"site-icon\" data-dominant-color=\"A18565\" width=\"192\" height=\"192\">\n\n      <a href=\"https://www.omgubuntu.co.uk/2024/04/neofetch-system-info-tool-is-dead\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow ugc\" title=\"03:10PM - 30 April 2024\">OMG! Ubuntu – 30 Apr 24</a>\n  </header>\n\n  <